WN.lb(7,{"mt_Z3G_97fnha":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In Tom's coat, who owns the coat?","choices":["Tom","The coat","Nobody"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word with 's. That word is the owner.","explain":"Tom has the 's, so Tom is the owner. The coat belongs to Tom."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence is right?","choices":["The girl's hat is blue.","The girls hat is blue.","The girl's like hats."],"answer":0,"hint":"One sentence shows a hat that belongs to a girl. One has the mark where nothing belongs.","explain":"The girl's hat shows the hat belongs to the girl. In 'The girl's like hats' nothing belongs, so it should be girls."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Say it the short way: the bed that belongs to Kim.","choices":["Kim's bed","bed's Kim","Kim bed"],"answer":0,"hint":"The owner comes first, then 's, then the thing.","explain":"Kim is the owner, so Kim's comes first, then the thing: Kim's bed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where does the apostrophe go to show the bone belongs to one dog?","choices":["dog's","dogs'","do'gs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write all of the owner word first, then the mark, then s.","explain":"Write dog, then the mark, then s: dog's. Putting the mark after the s would mean more than one dog."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A story has {a} words like dogs that just mean more than one, and {b} words like the dog's that show belonging. How many words do not need an apostrophe?","answer":"a","hint":"Words that only mean more than one need s but no mark.","explain":"The {a} words that just mean more than one take no apostrophe. Only the {b} belonging words need 's.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben wrote: I ate two apple's. What is the mistake?","choices":["Two apples just means more than one, so no apostrophe","It should be two apples'","There is no mistake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does anything belong to the apples?","explain":"Nothing belongs to the apples. More than one apple is just apples, with no mark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one needs an apostrophe added? The ___ tail is long.","choices":["cat's","cats","cat"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tail belongs to the cat. How do you show that?","explain":"The tail belongs to the cat, so write cat's: the cat's tail is long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam's bag has {n} books. He puts in {k} more. How many books are in Sam's bag now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Sam's bag is the bag that belongs to Sam. Add the new books.","explain":"{n} add {k} is {=n+k}, so the bag that belongs to Sam now has {=n+k} books.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The ball belongs to the boy. Which is right?","choices":["the boy's ball","the boys' ball","the boys ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the owner first: boy. Then the mark, then s.","explain":"One boy owns the ball, so write boy, then the apostrophe, then s: the boy's ball."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A list has {a} words that mean more than one, like cats. It has {b} words that show something belongs, like the cat's bed. How many words need an apostrophe?","answer":"b","hint":"Words that only mean more than one do not need the mark. Count only the belongs-to words.","explain":"Only the {b} belongs-to words need 's. The {a} words that just mean more than one need only s.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one means more than one cup, with nothing belonging?","choices":["two cups","two cup's","the cup's handle"],"answer":0,"hint":"More than one is just s. Is anything owned here?","explain":"Two cups means more than one cup. Nothing belongs to the cups, so there is no apostrophe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia's box has {n} pens in it. She gives {k} of them to Leo. How many pens are left in Mia's box?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Mia's box is the box that belongs to Mia. Start with {n} and take away {k}.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} pens are left in the box that belongs to Mia.","params":{"n":[4,9],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The dog has a bone. The bone belongs to the dog. We can write that the short way: the dog's bone.","The little mark is called an apostrophe. Write the owner first: dog. Then put the mark, then s: dog's. Then write the thing: bone.","Not every s needs the mark. Three cats ran means more than one cat. Nothing belongs to them, so there is no apostrophe. The cat's bed is the bed that belongs to the cat, so it has 's.","To find the owner, look for the word with 's. In Mia's cat, Mia has the 's. So the cat belongs to Mia."],"adult":["This is the possessive apostrophe with singular nouns. It is taught in Year 2 of the English National Curriculum (2014) as 'apostrophes to mark singular possession in nouns', for example the girl's name. In the US it sits in the Common Core Language standards for Grade 2 (L.2.2c: contractions and frequently occurring possessives). Plural possession (the girls' coats) comes later, in Year 4, so this lesson keeps to one owner.","The rule for one owner is always the same: write the whole noun, add an apostrophe, then add s. It works for names (Sam's), common nouns (the man's) and nouns that already end in s (the bus's door, James's bag; some style guides allow James'). The 's makes the noun possessive, and the owner comes before the thing owned: Ben's hat, never hat's Ben.","Common mistakes: an apostrophe in a plain plural (two apple's, often called the greengrocer's apostrophe); the mark after the s for one owner (the dogs' bone, which means more than one dog); leaving it out (the girls bag); and splitting the name (Sa'ms). Be careful with its. The possessive its (the dog wagged its tail) has no apostrophe, because it's always means it is. That is a contraction, a different use of the same mark.","In the lab your child drags the apostrophe into the gap between the owner and the s. Then they sort plain plurals from belongs-to words, read a phrase to find the owner, fix missing and wrong apostrophes, and squash 'the hat that belongs to Ben' into Ben's hat."]},"familyTip":"Go round the house naming things with 's: Dad's mug, the cat's bowl, Nana's chair. Write two or three on sticky notes together, and ask: who is the owner? Where does the mark go?","keyIdea":"An apostrophe and s ('s) show that something belongs to someone. Write the owner, then the apostrophe, then s, then the thing: the dog's bone, the girl's hat, Sam's kite. Do not use an apostrophe just because a word ends in s. Three cats means more than one cat, so it has no apostrophe.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it need an apostrophe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Just s: more than one","'s: belongs to"],"items":[["two dogs",0],["the dog's lead",1],["three hats",0],["Ali's hat",1],["some cakes",0],["the cake's icing",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagposs","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the apostrophe into the right gap, sort plurals from belongs-to words, find the owner, fix mistakes and say it the short way.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BhYJZUsErp":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tom writes “bd” for bed. What did he miss?","choices":["The middle sound e","The first sound b","A capital letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say bed slowly. What comes between b and d?","explain":"b … e … d. The middle vowel sound e was lost."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many sound boxes does “duck” need?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"ck makes one sound. It goes in one box.","explain":"d … u … ck. Four letters, but three sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the first sound in “ship”?","choices":["sh","s","h"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly. Do you hear s, or a shush sound?","explain":"s and h together make one sound, sh. Write sh in the first box."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word has 4 sounds?","choices":["frog","fog","dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tap a finger for each sound you hear.","explain":"f … r … o … g is four sounds. fog and dog have three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the last sound in “hand”?","choices":["d","n","a"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say hand slowly and listen to the very end.","explain":"h … a … n … d. n and d sit side by side, and d comes last."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which sound goes in the middle box of “pig”?","choices":["i","e","a"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stretch it: p … ? … g. Is it i as in ink?","explain":"p … i … g. With e it would say peg, and with a it would say pag."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Each word on a card has 4 sounds. There are {a} cards. How many sound boxes in all?","answer":"4*a","hint":"Each card needs 4 boxes. Count in fours.","explain":"{a} cards with 4 boxes each make {=4*a} boxes.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ben says the letter names “see, ay, tee”. What should he write for cat?","choices":["The sounds c … a … t","A letter for each name, seeaytee","Just c"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a word, we write the sound, not the letter name.","explain":"Say the sounds, not the names: c … a … t. One letter for each sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and ends in ck, which makes one sound. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sound boxes?","answer":"n-1","hint":"c and k share one box at the end. Take one away from the letters.","explain":"{n} letters, but ck fills one box, so {=n-1} boxes. Duck: 4 letters, 3 boxes.","params":{"n":[4,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many sounds are in “hen”?","choices":["3","2","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly and tap a finger for each sound.","explain":"h … e … n. Three sounds, so three boxes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Zara spells {a} words. Each word has 3 sounds. How many sound boxes does she draw?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Each word needs 3 boxes. Add 3 for every word.","explain":"{a} words with 3 boxes each make {=3*a} boxes.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which spelling of “stop” has every sound?","choices":["stop","sop","top"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stretch it: s … t … o … p. Is every sound there?","explain":"s and t sit side by side, and you hear both. sop and top each lose a sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them are sh, which makes one sound. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sound boxes?","answer":"n-1","hint":"sh fills one box, not two. Take one away from the letters.","explain":"{n} letters, but sh shares one box, so {=n-1} boxes. Fish: 4 letters, 3 boxes.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Blending is for reading. Segmenting is for spelling. To spell sun, say it slowly. Stretch it like a rubber band: s … u … n. That is three sounds.","Draw a box for each sound. Then write the letter for each sound in its box. s in the first box, u in the next, n in the last. Now you have sun.","Some sounds need two letters. In ship, sh is one sound. It goes in one box. So ship has 4 letters but 3 boxes: sh, i, p.","Do not lose a sound. The middle sound is easy to miss: cat needs its a. In frog, f and r sit side by side. Say it slowly and you hear both: f … r … o … g."],"adult":["Segmenting means splitting a spoken word into its phonemes (sounds) so a child can write a grapheme for each one. It is the spelling twin of blending, and English National Curriculum Year 1 expects children to spell words containing each of the 40+ phonemes taught by segmenting them. The US Common Core asks the same in L.K.2d and L.1.2e: spell simple words phonetically, drawing on phoneme-grapheme knowledge.","Sound boxes (Elkonin boxes) make the sounds visible: one box per phoneme, never one per letter. A digraph such as sh, ch or ck, or a double letter such as gg in egg, shares one box. Adjacent consonants, often called blends or consonant clusters (fr, st, nd), are two phonemes and need two boxes. CVC words come first, then CCVC and CVCC words such as frog, stop and hand.","Common mistakes: leaving out the medial vowel (ct for cat), dropping the second sound of a cluster (fog for frog, sop for stop), splitting a digraph across two boxes (s-h for sh), and writing letter names instead of sounds. Be careful to say pure sounds, mmm not muh, because an added uh sound makes children write extra letters.","In the lab your child picks the strip with one box per sound, drops the vowel into an empty middle box, puts back the lost sound in a blend, and spells whole words with tiles where sh, ck and ch fill one box. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play 'robot talk' at tea time. Say a word in sounds, like c … u … p, and ask your child to say the word. Then swap: they say a word in sounds for you. Tap one finger for each sound to count them.","keyIdea":"To spell a word, say it slowly and split it into its sounds. This is called segmenting. Give each sound its own box, then write the letter or letters for that sound: c-a-t, cat. Count sounds, not letters, so ship has 3 boxes (sh-i-p). Listen for the vowel sound in the middle, and in words like frog and tent, hear both sounds that sit side by side.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many sounds? Drag each word to the right number of sound boxes, or tap it and then tap a group.","opts":{"bins":["2 sounds","3 sounds","4 sounds"],"items":[["in",0],["up",0],["map",1],["chop",1],["ring",1],["stop",2],["milk",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsegbox","caption":"Flagship lab: count the sounds, fill the middle box, put back lost sounds, and spell words in sound boxes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_p_jxNLdus4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word spells the u sound with o?","choices":["Monday","Sunday","bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Find the u sound, then look at how it is spelled.","explain":"In Monday the u sound is spelled o. In Sunday and bus it is spelled u."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you spell the word that sounds like stay-shun, where trains stop?","choices":["station","stashun","stayshon"],"answer":0,"hint":"The shun sound at the end is spelled with four letters.","explain":"Station ends in tion. The shun sound at the end of a word like this is spelled t-i-o-n."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"“The waves crash in the ___.” Which word fits?","choices":["sea","see","si"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it the big water, or what your eyes do?","explain":"Sea is the big salty water. See is what your eyes do, with two e's like two eyes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which spelling is right for the round toy you kick?","choices":["ball","borl","bawl"],"answer":0,"hint":"The or sound comes right before ll.","explain":"Before l or ll the or sound is spelled a: ball, like all and call."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"“The children rode ___ bikes.” Which word fits?","choices":["their","there","they"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the bikes belong to the children?","explain":"Their means belonging to them: their bikes. There is a place word, like here."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Zak writes “muther”. What should he change?","choices":["Write o for the u sound: mother","Write uu: muuther","Leave it, it sounds right"],"answer":0,"hint":"In mother, which letter spells the u sound?","explain":"Mother is spelled with o, not u. It sounds like m-u-ther, but the u sound is written o, as in brother."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A list has {a} words ending in tion and {b} other words. How many words end with the shun sound spelled tion?","answer":"a","hint":"Only count the words that end in tion.","explain":"Only the {a} tion words end with shun spelled tion. The other {b} words do not count. So {=a}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"“Shh! Please be ___ in the library.” Which word fits?","choices":["quiet","quite","kwiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means not noisy?","explain":"Quiet means not noisy. Quite means a bit or very, as in quite big."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Mia finds {p} homophone pairs, like see and sea. How many words are in all her pairs?","answer":"2*p","hint":"Each pair has two words, so count two for every pair.","explain":"{p} pairs × 2 words = {=2*p} words, each with its own spelling and meaning.","params":{"p":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the right way to spell the word that means to move on your feet?","choices":["walk","work","wawk"],"answer":0,"hint":"The or sound comes just before l. Which letter spells it there?","explain":"Walk: before l, the or sound is spelled a. Work is a different word, and wawk is not a word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Asha writes {a} words where a says or, like ball, and {b} words where o says u, like mother. How many words is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two groups of words together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} words, each with a new spelling for a sound she already knew.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"“Can you ___ the bell?” Which word fits?","choices":["hear","here","heer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the word about a place, or about listening?","explain":"Hear is about listening, and it has ear in it. Here is a place word, like there and where."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben learns {p} homophone pairs, like see and sea, and {t} sets of three, like to, too and two. Every word has its own spelling. How many spellings is that?","answer":"2*p+3*t","hint":"A pair has 2 spellings and a set of three has 3. Add them all up.","explain":"{p} pairs give 2 × {p} spellings and {t} sets give 3 × {t}, so {=2*p+3*t} spellings in all.","params":{"p":[1,4],"t":[1,2]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You know the or sound in fork. In ball it is spelled a. When l or ll comes next, the or sound is often a: all, call, walk, talk.","You know the u sound in bus. In mother it is spelled o. Brother, nothing and Monday are the same. Say them, then look hard at the o.","Lots of long words end with shun. We write it tion. Station, potion and action all end in tion.","Some words sound the same but mean different things. They are homophones. You see with your eyes. Fish swim in the sea. You hear with your ear. Here is a place. Read the whole sentence, then pick the word that fits."],"adult":["This is the Year 2 spelling work in the English National Curriculum (Appendix 1): new graphemes for phonemes children can already spell, plus common homophones. In the US it sits under Common Core L.1.2d and L.2.2d, which ask children to spell common patterns and to generalise learned spelling patterns when writing. A phoneme is a sound; a grapheme is the letter or letters that write it; a homophone is a word that sounds like another but differs in spelling and meaning.","The patterns here are /ɔː/ spelled a before l and ll (all, ball, call, walk, talk, always), /ʌ/ spelled o (other, mother, brother, nothing, Monday), the ending /ʃən/ spelled tion (station, fiction, motion, section, national), and the homophone pairs see and sea, here and hear, there and their, quite and quiet. The a before l rule is a strong pattern, not a law: fall and stall follow it, but pal and shall do not, so it is taught through word families.","Common mistakes: spelling ball as borl or bawl because or and aw are the spellings already learned; writing muther for mother; writing the sound shun instead of the spelling tion; and choosing a homophone by sound alone, such as I can here you. A helpful habit is to say the whole sentence and ask what the word means before choosing. Be careful not to say that one spelling is always right for a sound; each one belongs to its own set of words.","In the lab your child drops a, or or aw into a gap, builds mother and Monday from parts with a u decoy in the tray, hooks the tion carriage onto a word train, and finishes sentences with the right homophone. Every wrong pick explains which spelling belongs where."]},"familyTip":"Make a homophone pair card: write see and sea on one card, with an eye drawn in the two e's of see and a wave under sea. Each day, say a sentence out loud and let your child point to the word that fits. Then swap: they say a sentence for you.","keyIdea":"Some sounds you already know have a new spelling in certain words. The or sound is spelled a before l or ll (all, ball, walk, talk). The u sound is spelled o in other, mother, brother, nothing and Monday. The shun sound at the end of a word is spelled tion (station, motion, fiction). Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings, such as see and sea, here and hear, there and their, so the meaning of the sentence tells you which spelling to write.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which new spelling? Drag each word to its box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["or sound spelled a","u sound spelled o","shun spelled tion"],"items":[["all",0],["small",0],["other",1],["Monday",1],["fiction",2],["section",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtwin","caption":"Flagship lab: drop a into ball, build mother from parts, hook tion onto a train, and pick the right homophone.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_37QCuGOxFe":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What are homophones?","choices":["Words that sound the same but mean different things","Words that mean the same thing","Words that rhyme with each other"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of brake and break.","explain":"Homophones sound exactly the same but have different meanings and spellings, like brake and break."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word fits? Squeeze the ___ to stop.","choices":["brake","break","brayk"],"answer":0,"hint":"You want the part of a bike that stops it.","explain":"brake is the stopping part. break means to smash, or a rest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word fits? I think ___ lost.","choices":["they're","their","there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the swap test: does 'they are' fit?","explain":"I think they are lost, so write they're."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which clue helps you spell hear?","choices":["You hear with your ear","here is a place","It rhymes with deer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a small word hidden inside hear.","explain":"hear has ear inside it, and you hear with your ear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Ella writes {n} sentences. She fixes {k} wrong homophones. The rest were right. How many were right?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the fixed sentences away from all of them.","explain":"{n} sentences take away {k} fixed leaves {=n-k} that were right.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the number after seven?","choices":["eight","ate","ayt"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is spelt with eigh, like weigh.","explain":"The number is eight. ate is the past of eat, and ayt is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which sentence is spelt right?","choices":["Let's meet at the park.","Let's meat at the park.","Let's mete at the park."],"answer":0,"hint":"meat is food. Which word means come together?","explain":"meet means come together. You eat meat, so meat is the food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word means calm, with no fighting?","choices":["peace","piece","peece"],"answer":0,"hint":"piece has pie in it, so it is the part one.","explain":"peace means calm. piece means a part, like a piece of pie."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word fits? A ___ flew over the town.","choices":["plane","plain","plan"],"answer":0,"hint":"You want a flying machine.","explain":"plane is a flying machine. plain means simple, or flat land."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A word bank has {n} homophone pairs, like plain and plane. Tom learns {k} pairs this week. How many pairs are left to learn?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the pairs Tom has learned away from all the pairs.","explain":"{n} pairs take away {k} learned leaves {=n-k} pairs to learn.","params":{"n":[7,12],"k":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word fits? I ate a ___ of cake.","choices":["piece","peace","peice"],"answer":0,"hint":"A part of something. Think: a piece of pie.","explain":"piece means a part of something. peace means calm, and peice is not a word."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam finds {n} pairs of homophones, like meat and meet. How many words is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each pair has two words that sound the same.","explain":"Two words in each pair, so {n} pairs make {=2*n} words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word fits? ___ dog is barking.","choices":["Their","There","They're"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask who owns the dog. It belongs to them.","explain":"Their shows the dog belongs to them. There is a place, and They're means they are."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A list has {n} words with the ay sound. {k} of them are spelt with eigh, like eight. The rest use ey, like they. How many use ey?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the eigh words away from the whole list.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} eigh words leaves {=n-k} ey words.","params":{"n":[8,12],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is spelt right?","choices":["They are late.","Thay are late.","Thei are late."],"answer":0,"hint":"The ay sound in this word is spelt e, y.","explain":"they uses ey for the ay sound, like grey and obey."}],"explain":{"kid":["Say brake and break out loud. They sound just the same. But a brake stops your bike, and to break means to smash. Words like this are called homophones. You can't hear which one to write, so think about what the sentence means.","Look for a clue inside the word. You hear with your ear. You eat meat. A piece of pie. Here, there and where are all place words.","Three words trip up lots of writers. there is a place. their means it belongs to them. they're means they are. If 'they are' fits, write they're.","The sound ay has some odd spellings too. It is ei in vein, eigh in eight and weigh, and ey in they and grey."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. The National Curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Years 3 and 4) gives a list of homophones and near-homophones to learn. It includes brake/break, grate/great, here/hear, heel/heal, meat/meet, peace/piece, plain/plane, rain/rein/reign, scene/seen, weather/whether and whose/who's. The same list teaches the ay sound spelt ei (vein), eigh (eight, weigh, sleigh) and ey (they, obey, grey). there, their and they're come from Year 2 and are revisited here.","Homophones sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning, so phonics alone cannot pick between them. The habit to build is to read the whole sentence, decide what the word means, then choose the spelling. Clues inside the spelling help a lot: ear in hear, eat in meat, pie in piece, here in there and where. The 'they are' swap test sorts they're from there and their.","Common mistakes: choosing by sound alone (I can here you, press the break); always writing the more familiar word of a pair; swapping letters, as in peice; using their for 'there is'; and spelling the ay sound by ear, as in thay or ayt. Be careful with near-homophones such as accept and except, which sound alike but not the same.","In the lab your child picks the spelling that matches a picture sentence, sorts six there, their and they're gaps with the swap test, taps the clue letters hidden in five words, builds six ay words from ei, eigh or ey, and fixes four sentences with a wrong homophone. Each wrong move says why, for example 'peace means calm'."]},"familyTip":"Play 'which one?' at dinner. Say a sentence such as 'Pass me a piece of bread' and ask your child to write or point to the right spelling: piece or peace. Then hunt for the hidden clue together: pie in piece, ear in hear, eat in meat. For there, their and they're, teach the swap test: if 'they are' fits the gap, write they're.","keyIdea":"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different meanings and spellings, such as brake and break, piece and peace, or meat and meet. You cannot hear which one is meant, so you choose the spelling from the meaning of the sentence. Clues hidden inside a spelling help: you hear with your ear, you eat meat, and a piece of pie. there is a place, their means it belongs to them, and they're is short for they are. The sound ay can also be spelt ei (vein), eigh (eight, weigh) or ey (they, grey).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How is the ay sound spelt? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["ei, as in vein","eigh, as in eight","ey, as in they"],"items":[["rein",0],["weigh",1],["grey",2],["veil",0],["sleigh",1],["obey",2],["neighbour",1],["prey",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghphone","caption":"Flagship lab: a picture picks the spelling, sort there/their/they're, find clues inside words, build ay words with ei, eigh, ey, and fix mix-ups.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6W5zzDIGZH":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which list is in alphabetical order?","choices":["shark, shed, ship","ship, shed, shark","shed, shark, ship"],"answer":0,"hint":"All start with sh. Look at the third letter.","explain":"The third letters are a, e and i, so shark, shed, ship."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What do the guide words on a page show?","choices":["The first and last words on the page","The hardest words on the page","The only words on the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the two words at the top of a dictionary page.","explain":"Guide words are the first and last words on the page. Every word between them is there too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Guide words: bad and bell. Your word is bus. Which way do you turn?","choices":["Later","Earlier","Stay on this page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does bus come before bad or after bell?","explain":"bus comes after bell because u comes after e, so turn later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You wrote beutiful. Which letters do you look up first?","choices":["be","ul","ful"],"answer":0,"hint":"A dictionary is ordered by the start of the word.","explain":"Look up be, then check each letter: the entry is beautiful."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Raj checks {n} words in the dictionary. {k} were spelt wrong. How many were spelt right?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the wrong ones away from all the words he checked.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} wrong leaves {=n-k} spelt right.","params":{"n":[6,12],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the dictionary spelling?","choices":["friend","freind","frend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check every letter: f, r, i, e, n, d.","explain":"The entry is friend: i comes before e. freind swaps them and frend misses the i."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A dictionary has {n} pages. You open it in the middle. About which page is that?","answer":"n/2","hint":"The middle is half of all the pages.","explain":"Half of {n} pages is {=n/2}, so the middle is about page {=n/2}.","params":{"n":[100,300]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"fone is not in the dictionary. Which spelling should you try?","choices":["phone","foan","fhone"],"answer":0,"hint":"The f sound can be spelt with two letters.","explain":"The f sound in phone is spelt ph, so look under ph."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"The entry starts the same as your word. What must you do?","choices":["Check every letter matches","Copy the first word you see","Look only at the last letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"fright and friend both start with fri.","explain":"Words can start the same and still be different, so check every letter."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word comes first in a dictionary?","choices":["cake","city","crab"],"answer":0,"hint":"All start with c. Compare the second letters: a, i and r.","explain":"a comes before i and r, so cake comes first, then city, then crab."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A dictionary page has {n} words. The two guide words are the first and last. How many words are between them?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the first word and the last word.","explain":"{n} words take away the 2 guide words leaves {=n-2} words between them.","params":{"n":[8,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The guide words are frog and fun. Which word is on this page?","choices":["fruit","fan","gate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the word after frog and before fun?","explain":"fruit comes after frog (u after o) and before fun (r before u). fan is earlier and gate is later."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Kim is on page {a}. The word she wants is on page {b}. How many pages does she turn forward?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Count on from page {a} to page {b}.","explain":"From page {a} to page {b} is {=b-a} pages.","params":{"a":[10,30],"b":[40,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You look up nife and it is not there. What should you try next?","choices":["kn, as in know","give up","look under ni again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some words start with a silent letter.","explain":"knife starts with a silent k, so look under kn."}],"explain":{"kid":["A dictionary puts words in ABC order. That means you can find any word fast. Look at its first letter, then its second, then its third. bag comes before bed, because a comes before e.","At the top of each page are two guide words. They are the first and last words on that page. If your word comes between them, it is on that page. If it comes before, turn back. If it comes after, turn on.","Not sure how to spell a word? Look up its first two or three letters. Find the word, then check every letter. You might write freind, but the dictionary shows friend.","Can't find it at all? The first sound might be spelt another way. skool is not there, but school is, with sch. Try kn for knife, ph for phone and wr for write."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 11. The National Curriculum in England asks pupils in Years 3 and 4 to 'use the first two or three letters of a word to check its spelling in a dictionary'. In the US, Common Core L.2.2e and L.3.2g ask children to consult reference materials, including beginning dictionaries, to check and correct spellings. Key terms: alphabetical order, guide words (also called catchwords) and entry (the headword and its meaning).","The skill has three parts. First, alphabetical order beyond the first letter: when first letters match, compare the second, then the third. Second, guide words: a word belongs on a page if it falls between the two guide words, so a child can decide to turn earlier or later instead of reading every page. Third, checking: compare the written word with the entry letter by letter.","Common mistakes: ordering by the first letter only, so bus is placed before bag; thinking a word must be a guide word to be on the page; flicking through from the front; stopping when an entry starts the same, such as picking fright for friend; and deciding a word does not exist when the first sound is spelt another way (skool, nife, fone, rite). Be careful with that last one, as the fix is to try another spelling of the sound.","In the lab your child taps three lists of words into order using the second letter, files six words onto pages by their guide words, turns pages earlier or later to find five words, picks the right entry for four misspellings, and tries sch, kn, ph or wr when a word seems to be missing. Each wrong move says why, for example 'egg comes before fan, so it is on an earlier page'."]},"familyTip":"Keep a children's dictionary next to homework. When your child is unsure of a spelling, ask 'what are the first two letters?' and let them find the page using the guide words rather than flicking from the front. Race each other: one of you calls out a word, the other says 'earlier' or 'later' at each page until it is found. If a word seems to be missing, talk about other ways to write the first sound.","keyIdea":"A dictionary lists words in alphabetical order, so you can find a word by its first two or three letters. When the first letters match, the next letter decides the order: bag comes before bed. The two guide words at the top of each page show the first and last word on it, so any word that comes between them is on that page. To check a spelling, look up the first letters, then compare every letter with the entry: freind is wrong, the entry says friend. If the word is not there, the first sound may be spelt another way, such as sch in school, kn in knife, ph in phone or wr in write.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which page is each word on? Use the guide words. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a page.","opts":{"bins":["Page: sad to shy","Page: sit to sun","Page: swan to syrup"],"items":[["seed",0],["sock",1],["sweet",2],["shell",0],["star",1],["swim",2],["sand",0],["stop",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdict","caption":"Flagship lab: order words by the second letter, file words between guide words, turn pages to a word, check a spelling, and try new first letters.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zBUcAPDRPM":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What do you do when you unpack a bag?","choices":["take things out","put things in","pack it again"],"answer":0,"hint":"un- undoes the job. What does pack do?","explain":"Packing puts things in, so unpacking undoes it: you take things out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does un- go?","choices":["at the front of the word","at the end of the word","in the middle of the word"],"answer":0,"hint":"un- is a prefix. Pre means before.","explain":"A prefix goes before the word: un + kind = unkind."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The cat is not well. The cat is ...","choices":["unwell","well","wellun"],"answer":0,"hint":"not well needs un- at the front.","explain":"unwell means not well, with un- at the front."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"There are {a} zips done up on the bags. Mia unzips {b}. How many are still done up?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Each one she unzips is open now.","explain":"{a} done up take away {b} opened leaves {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is spelt right? (not kind)","choices":["unkind","unkine","un kind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep every letter of kind and leave no gap.","explain":"un + kind = unkind, with kind spelt just the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is under NOT an un- word?","choices":["Take off un and der is not a word","It is too long","It has an e in it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take off un. What is left?","explain":"Take un off under and der is left, which is not a word. unfair leaves fair, so it has the prefix."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A list has {k} words like kind, fair and tidy. Sam adds un- to each one. How many un- words does he make?","answer":"k","hint":"One un- word for each word on the list.","explain":"Each word gives one un- word, so {k}.","params":{"k":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Gran helps me tie my shoes. That is ...","choices":["kind","unkind","unhelp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is helping a nice thing to do?","explain":"Helping is nice, so it is kind. unkind would mean not kind, and unhelp is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"You undo your coat button. What happens?","choices":["It comes open","It gets done up","It falls off"],"answer":0,"hint":"undo undoes do. What did doing up the button do?","explain":"un- undoes the job, so the button comes open."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which one means not tidy?","choices":["untidy","tidyun","retidy"],"answer":0,"hint":"un- goes at the front and means not.","explain":"untidy means not tidy. un- goes at the front."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does unfair mean?","choices":["not fair","very fair","fair again"],"answer":0,"hint":"un- in front of a describing word means not.","explain":"un + fair = unfair, which means not fair."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {h} letters. You put un- in front of it. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"h+2","hint":"un adds two letters, u and n, and the word stays the same.","explain":"The word keeps all {h} letters and un adds 2, so {=h+2} letters.","params":{"h":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is spelt right?","choices":["unlock","un lock","onlock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write u, n, then the word, with no gap.","explain":"un joins straight onto lock: unlock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ali ties {t} bows. Then he unties {u} of them. How many bows are still tied?","answer":"t-u","hint":"untie undoes tie, so each one he unties is not tied any more.","explain":"{t} tied take away {u} untied leaves {=t-u} still tied.","params":{"t":[5,8],"u":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word has the prefix un-?","choices":["untidy","under","uncle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take off un. Is a real word left?","explain":"untidy is un + tidy. Take un off under or uncle and no word is left."}],"explain":{"kid":["un is a small part we put at the front of a word. It is called a prefix. It changes what the word means.","Put un in front of happy. Now you have unhappy. It means not happy. unkind means not kind. unfair means not fair.","un can undo a job too. You lock the door. Then you unlock it, and it opens. You tie a bow. Then you untie it.","To spell it, write u, n, then the word. Do not change the word. No gap. Be careful: under and uncle just start with u and n. Take off un and no word is left."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. The National Curriculum in England lists the prefix un- in Year 1 spelling (English Appendix 1: 'The prefix un- is added at the beginning of a word without any change to the spelling of the root word', with unhappy, undo, unload, unfair and unlock as examples). Reading the same words is part of Year 1 word reading, and Common Core L.1.4b and L.2.4b use affixes as clues to word meaning.","un- does two jobs. On an adjective it means not (unhappy, unkind, untidy, unwell). On a verb it reverses the action (unlock, untie, unzip, unpack, undo). It is a prefix because it comes before the root word, and the root keeps every letter, so unnecessary has two n's only because necessary starts with n.","Common mistakes: putting the prefix after the word (happyun), leaving a gap (un happy), dropping or doubling a root letter (unhapy, unzipp), writing on- for un-, reading un- as 'very' or 'again', and treating any word that starts with u-n (under, uncle, unicorn) as un- plus a word. Children also invent words such as unbig; not every word takes un-, and the opposite of big is small.","In the lab your child drags un- to the front of four describing words, taps the picture that shows unlock, untie, unzip and unpack, picks the right spelling of four un- words, sorts six words by taking un off to see if a real word is left, and fills four sentence gaps where one gap needs the plain word kind."]},"familyTip":"Play 'flip it' at home. You say a word, such as happy, kind, tidy or zip, and your child adds un- and says what the new word means: unzip opens the zip. Then write two of them together, saying 'un, then the whole word'. If they say unbig or unsad, laugh together and find the real opposite: small, happy.","keyIdea":"un- is a prefix: a small part that goes at the front of a word and changes what it means. With a describing word, un- means not: unhappy means not happy, unfair means not fair. With a doing word, un- undoes the job: unlock opens what lock shut, untie undoes a bow. To spell it, write un and then the whole word with no gap and no letters changed. Some words just start with u and n, like under and uncle; take off un and no word is left, so they have no prefix.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does un- mean not, or does it undo a job? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["means not (unkind)","undoes a job (unlock)"],"items":[["unhappy",0],["unlock",1],["unfair",0],["untie",1],["untidy",0],["unzip",1],["unwell",0],["unpack",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagunpre","caption":"Flagship lab: put un- at the front, match un- words to pictures, spell them, spot words that only start with un, and fill sentence gaps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QdMMLRYWhn":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is spelt right? (it sounds like jump-t)","choices":["jumped","jumpt","jumpd"],"answer":0,"hint":"The past ending is always spelt the same way.","explain":"It sounds like t, but the past ending is spelt ed: jumped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the root word in helping?","choices":["help","helpi","hel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take off the ending -ing. A real word is left.","explain":"helping = help + ing. The root help did not change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Look! The dog is ... the ball.","choices":["kicking","kicked","kicks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look! means it is happening now.","explain":"It is happening now, so is + kicking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam writes {a} words that end in -ed and {b} words that end in -ing. How many words have an ending?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the -ed words and the -ing words.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} words with an ending.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"He ... the box. (every day)","choices":["fixes","fixs","fixed"],"answer":0,"hint":"fix ends in x. Every day takes the -s or -es ending.","explain":"After x you add -es: he fixes the box every day."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia is ... than Ben.","choices":["taller","tallest","more taller"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two people are compared, so which ending means more?","explain":"For two, use -er: taller. It already means more, so do not add more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is spelt right? (it sounds like want-id)","choices":["wanted","wantid","wantd"],"answer":0,"hint":"The past ending is still e, d, even with the extra beat.","explain":"want + ed = wanted. It has an extra beat, but it is spelt ed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{n} towers stand in a row. Each one is taller than the one before. How many towers are taller than the first one?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Every tower after the first one is taller than it.","explain":"All but the first are taller, so {=n-1}. The last one is the tallest.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is right?","choices":["She is singing.","She is singin.","She is singging."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep sing whole and write all of i, n, g.","explain":"sing + ing = singing. No extra g and no missing g."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"What does -est mean in quickest?","choices":["the most quick of all","a bit quick","quick in the past"],"answer":0,"hint":"-er is more. -est is the most.","explain":"quickest means the most quick of all of them."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Yesterday we ... at the park.","choices":["played","playd","playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yesterday is in the past. The past ending is spelt e, d.","explain":"play + ed = played. It sounds like d at the end, but it is spelt ed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A root word has {r} letters, like jump. You add -ing and change nothing else. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"r+3","hint":"The root keeps all its letters. Then count i, n, g.","explain":"The root keeps its {r} letters and -ing adds 3, so {=r+3}. jump has 4, jumping has 7.","params":{"r":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Every day Mum ... the dishes.","choices":["washes","washs","wash"],"answer":0,"hint":"wash ends in sh. What do you add after sh?","explain":"After s, x, z, sh or ch you add -es, so Mum washes the dishes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A root word has {r} letters, like help. You add -ed. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"r+2","hint":"No letters change. -ed adds e and d.","explain":"{r} letters + 2 for e and d = {=r+2}. help has 4, helped has 6.","params":{"r":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is right? (three snakes)","choices":["This one is the longest.","This one is the longer.","This one is the longist."],"answer":0,"hint":"Out of three, the most of all takes -est.","explain":"longest picks out the most of all three. It is spelt e, s, t."}],"explain":{"kid":["A root word is the main word, like jump. We can add an ending to it. The ending tells us more.","Now I am jumping. Yesterday I jumped. Every day Sam jumps. The root jump stays the same each time.","The ed ending can sound like t, like in jumped. It can sound like d, like in played. It is still spelt e, d.","Some words end in x, s or sh. They take es, like fix and fixes. You can hear the extra beat. Add er to say more. Add est to say the most of all. tall, taller, tallest."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. English Appendix 1 for Year 1 lists adding -s and -es to words (plural nouns and the third person singular of verbs), adding -ing, -ed and -er to verbs where no change is needed to the root word (hunting, hunted, hunter; buzzing, buzzed, buzzer; jumping, jumped, jumper), and adding -er and -est to adjectives where no change is needed (grander, grandest; fresher, freshest; quicker, quickest). Common Core L.1.1e and L.1.1f cover the matching grammar: verbs for past, present and future, and frequently occurring adjectives.","The key spelling idea is that the root word is written whole and unchanged, then the suffix. The past tense -ed has three sounds, /t/ after voiceless sounds (jumped, helped), /d/ after voiced sounds (played), and /ɪd/ after t or d (wanted), but one spelling. -es is used after s, x, z, sh and ch, where it adds a syllable (fix-es). -er compares two things and -est picks out one from three or more.","Common mistakes: spelling -ed as it sounds (jumpt, playd, wantid), dropping the g from -ing (jumpin), doubling a letter that should not be doubled (jumpping), writing fixs, adding more to a word that already has -er (more taller), using -est for two things, and spelling -est as ist. Words that do change their root, such as hopping, hoping and cried, belong to the next step (Suffixes), so this lesson only uses roots that need no change.","In the lab your child drags -ing, -ed or -s onto five roots to match a time clue, picks the spelling of four -ed words from how they sound, taps towers and snakes in the order tall, taller, tallest, cuts six words back to their root, and finishes four sentences, one of which needs -es."]},"familyTip":"Play 'now, yesterday, every day'. Pick a doing word such as jump, kick or help. Your child says it three ways: Now I am jumping. Yesterday I jumped. Every day I jump. Then write one of them together and circle the root. If they write jumpt, say the word slowly and remind them: the past ending is always spelt e, d.","keyIdea":"An ending is a few letters added to the end of a root word. -ing tells you something is happening now (jumping), -ed tells you it happened in the past (helped), and -s tells you it happens again and again (Sam plays). Words that end in s, x, z, sh or ch take -es instead (fixes, wishes). -er means more and -est means the most of all (taller, tallest). With these words the root is not changed at all: write the whole root, then the ending. -ed is always spelt ed, even when it sounds like t (jumped) or d (played).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it happening now, or did it happen in the past? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["now (-ing)","in the past (-ed)"],"items":[["jumping",0],["helped",1],["looking",0],["played",1],["kicking",0],["wanted",1],["singing",0],["picked",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvend","caption":"Flagship lab: add the ending a time clue needs, spell -ed by rule, label tall, taller, tallest, and cut words to the root.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FYK8m6eHQm":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is run + ing?","choices":["running","runing","runeing"],"answer":0,"hint":"run has one short vowel and ends in one consonant.","explain":"Double the n, then add ing: running."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is jump + ing?","choices":["jumping","jumpping","jumpeing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last two letters of jump. Are they both consonants?","explain":"jump ends in m and p, two consonants, so nothing doubles: jumping."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is cry + ing?","choices":["crying","criing","cryeing"],"answer":0,"hint":"The y to i rule has one ending where the y stays.","explain":"The y stays before -ing, so there is no double i: crying."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is happy + ly?","choices":["happily","happyly","happly"],"answer":0,"hint":"happy ends in p and then y. Change the y before adding ly.","explain":"y turns to i before -ly: happily."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and ends in a consonant then y, like copy. You add -ed. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+2","hint":"The y turns into i, so the root keeps the same number of letters. Then add e and d.","explain":"The root still has {n} letters (y became i), and ed adds 2, so {=n+2} letters.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A root word has {n} letters. You add -ness and nothing changes, like sad and sadness. How many letters now?","answer":"n+4","hint":"-ness has four letters: n, e, s, s.","explain":"{n} letters plus 4 for ness gives {=n+4} letters.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which rule made baked?","choices":["drop the e","double the last letter","change y to i"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the root inside baked. How does the root end?","explain":"The root is bake. The e went before -ed, so the rule is drop the e."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is play + ed?","choices":["played","plaied","playd"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a vowel or a consonant just before the y in play?","explain":"a comes before the y, so the y stays and you just add ed: played."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is big + est?","choices":["biggest","bigest","bigggest"],"answer":0,"hint":"big has one short vowel and ends in one consonant.","explain":"Double the g once, then add est: biggest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"What does hopeless mean?","choices":["without hope","full of hope","hoping again"],"answer":0,"hint":"-less means without. -ful means full of.","explain":"hope + less = hopeless, which means without hope. The e stays because -less starts with a consonant."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is hope + ing?","choices":["hoping","hopeing","hopping"],"answer":0,"hint":"-ing starts with a vowel. What happens to the e at the end of hope?","explain":"The e goes before a vowel ending: hope + ing = hoping. hopping is a different word, from hop."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A root word has {n} letters and ends in e, like smile. You add -ing. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+2","hint":"First the e goes. Then you add the three letters i, n and g.","explain":"{n} letters take away the e leaves {=n-1}. Adding i, n, g gives {=n+2} letters.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is cry + ed?","choices":["cried","cryed","cride"],"answer":0,"hint":"cry ends in a consonant and then y. What does the y turn into?","explain":"Change y to i, then add ed: cried."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A short word has {n} letters, one short vowel, and ends in one consonant, like hop. You add -ing. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+4","hint":"Double the last letter, which adds one. Then add i, n and g.","explain":"{n} letters, plus 1 for the double letter, plus 3 for ing, gives {=n+4} letters.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word is spelt right?","choices":["careful","carful","carefull"],"answer":0,"hint":"-ful starts with f, a consonant. Does care change?","explain":"care keeps its e before a consonant ending, and the suffix -ful has one l: careful."}],"explain":{"kid":["A suffix is an ending. We add it to a root word. hope can be hoping, hoped or hopeful. Most of the time we just add it.","Rule 1. Some words end in e, like hope. If the ending starts with a, e, i, o or u, the e goes. hope and ing make hoping.","Rule 2. Some short words end in one vowel and one more letter, like run. Write the last letter twice. run and ing make running. hop and ed make hopped.","Rule 3. Some words end in y, like cry. The y turns into i: cried, happily. But keep the y for ing: crying. Endings like ful and ness do not change the word: careful, sadness."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. The National Curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Year 2 spelling) lists adding -ing, -ed, -er, -est and -y to words ending in -e with a consonant before it (hiking, hiked, hiker, nicer, nicest, shiny); adding the same endings to words of one syllable ending in a single consonant letter after a single vowel letter (patting, humming, sadder, fattest, runny); changing y to i before -ed, -er and -est (copied, happier, happiest) but not before -ing (copying, crying); and adding -ment, -ness, -ful, -less and -ly (enjoyment, sadness, careful, playful, hopeless, badly). Common Core L.1.2d and L.2.2d cover the same conventional spellings.","Each rule protects a sound. Dropping the e stops a clash of vowels (hopeing). Doubling the consonant keeps the vowel short: hopping has a short o, hoping a long one. The y to i change applies only when a consonant comes before the y, so play becomes played, not plaied, and the y stays before -ing so that ii never appears. Consonant suffixes need no change, except that consonant + y still turns into i: happiness, happily.","Common mistakes: keeping the e (hopeing, makeing), dropping the e before a consonant ending (carful, hopless), not doubling (runing, hoped for hopped), doubling after two consonants or two vowels (jumpping, rainning), writing cryed or happyly, and writing criing or flieing because the y to i rule was used before -ing.","In the lab your child taps the letter that changes, or taps just add, for four drop-e words, five doubling words, five y words and five -ful, -less, -ness and -ment words, then sorts eight finished words by the rule that made them."]},"familyTip":"Pick one root a day, such as hope, hop or cry, and build its family together: hoping, hoped, hopeful. Ask your child to say the rule out loud before writing: 'it ends in e and -ing starts with a vowel, so the e goes'. Write hoping and hopping side by side and talk about how the double p keeps the o short.","keyIdea":"A suffix is an ending added to a root word, such as -ing, -ed, -er, -est, -ful, -less, -ness, -ment and -ly. Most roots just take the ending. Three rules change the root first. If the root ends in e and the ending starts with a vowel, drop the e: hope, hoping. If the root has one short vowel and ends in one consonant, double that consonant before a vowel ending: run, running. If the root ends in a consonant then y, change y to i before every ending except -ing: cry, cried, but crying. Endings that start with a consonant, like -ful and -ness, keep the e and do not double: hopeful, sadness.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which rule made each word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["just add","drop the e","double the last letter","change y to i"],"items":[["jumping",0],["hoping",1],["running",2],["cried",3],["careful",0],["baked",1],["biggest",2],["happily",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsfxrule","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the letter that changes when you add an ending (drop e, double, y to i) or just add, then sort words by rule.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Jq0MjURrRC":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is spelt right?","choices":["school","skool","scool"],"answer":0,"hint":"The k sound in school is spelt in an odd way.","explain":"school spells the k sound with ch. skool and scool are how it sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"We had fun ___ it was sunny.","choices":["because","becos","becuase"],"answer":0,"hint":"It sounds like becos. The real word has au and an e on the end.","explain":"because is b, e, c, a, u, s, e. becos is spelt by sound, and becuase has the u and a swapped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which day is spelt right?","choices":["Saturday","Saterday","Satday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: Sat, ur, day.","explain":"Saturday has ur in the middle. Saterday uses the wrong letters, and Satday misses a part."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the best way to learn a tricky word?","choices":["Look, cover, write, check","Sound out each letter","Copy it 10 times fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sounding out gives the wrong spelling for a tricky word.","explain":"Look, cover, write, check makes you spell it from memory. Copying fast does not make you remember."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"could, would and should share a trick. What is it?","choices":["they end in ould","they start with c","they have oo"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the end of each word.","explain":"All three end in ould, which says ood. The l is not heard."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam learns {n} tricky words each week. How many does Sam learn in {w} weeks?","answer":"n*w","hint":"Each week adds {n} more words. Count up {n} at a time, {w} times.","explain":"{n} words a week for {w} weeks is {=n*w} words. A few words each week sticks better than lots at once.","params":{"n":[1,3],"w":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Come and play. Which word has the same trick as come?","choices":["some","cup","cone"],"answer":0,"hint":"In come, the o says u. Find another o that says u.","explain":"some has an o that says u and an e at the end, like come. cup uses u, and cone has a long o."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"My ___ is kind.","choices":["friend","frend","freind"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is an i before the e.","explain":"friend is f, r, i, e, n, d. frend is spelt by sound, and freind puts e before i."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word sounds out with no tricky part?","choices":["dog","was","said"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Do the letters match the sounds?","explain":"dog is d, o, g, as it sounds. was sounds like wos and said sounds like sed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"There are 7 days in a week. Ana can spell {n} of the day names. How many does she still need to learn?","answer":"7-n","hint":"Take the days she knows away from all 7 days.","explain":"7 days take away the {n} she knows leaves {=7-n} to learn. Start with the tricky ones, like Wednesday.","params":{"n":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mum ___ yes. Which spelling is right?","choices":["said","sed","siad"],"answer":0,"hint":"It sounds like sed, but it is a tricky word. Look at the middle.","explain":"said is s, a, i, d. sed is how it sounds, and siad has the a and i the wrong way round."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A tricky word has {n} letters. Only one part, 2 letters long, is tricky. How many letters spell the usual way?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take the 2 tricky letters away from all the letters.","explain":"{n} letters take away 2 tricky letters leaves {=n-2}. In said, only ai is tricky, so s and d spell the usual way.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which day comes after Tuesday?","choices":["Wednesday","Wensday","Thursday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the days in order. Then check for the d you cannot hear.","explain":"After Tuesday comes Wednesday. It has a d after the We. Wensday leaves it out, and Thursday comes later."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Monday is school day 1 and Friday is school day 5. Today is school day {n}. How many school days come after today?","answer":"5-n","hint":"There are 5 school days. Count the days after today.","explain":"5 school days, and you are on day {n}, so {=5-n} more come after today.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which part of friend is the tricky part?","choices":["ie","fr","nd"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which letters do not spell the sound the usual way?","explain":"fr and nd sound out. The ie says e, which is not the usual way to spell that sound."}],"explain":{"kid":["Most words spell the way they sound. Say cat: c, a, t. Write c, a, t. Done.","Some words do not. Say said. It sounds like sed. But we write s, a, i, d. Words like this are tricky words.","Only one part is tricky. In said, the s and the d are fine. Only the ai is odd. In school, only the ch is odd.","To learn one: look at it, cover it, write it, then check. Do it again on another day. The days of the week are tricky too. They all end in day. Wednesday has a d we do not say."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. The National Curriculum in England calls these common exception words: words where the usual grapheme for a sound is not used, or where a rule not yet taught applies. Year 1 includes said, says, was, were, come, some, love, friend, school, put, push, pull and our; Year 2 adds door, eye, could, should, would, because, pretty, any, many, people and water. Children also learn to spell the days of the week in Year 1. In US terms this sits with Common Core L.1.2d and L.2.2d (spelling common irregular words).","Tricky does not mean the whole word is irregular. In said, only the ai is unusual; s and d follow the rules. Teachers often mark the tricky part (sometimes with a heart) and teach the rest by phonics. For weekdays, the regular ending day helps, and some starts carry history: Wednesday comes from Woden's day, so it keeps a d that is no longer spoken.","Common mistakes: spelling by sound alone (sed, wos, cum, skool, becos, frend), leaving out a silent letter (Wensday, cud), getting the letters right but in the wrong order (siad, freind), and practising a word once and expecting it to stick. Short, spaced practice with look, cover, write, check works better than copying a word ten times in a row.","In the lab your child swaps the one wrong part of five sound-it-out spellings (skool to school), writes four words from memory after covering them, builds all seven day names, finds the misspelt tricky word in five sentences, and sorts words into two families that share a trick (could, would, should; come, some, Monday)."]},"familyTip":"Pick three tricky words a week, like said, come and friend. Your child looks at one, says the letters, covers it, writes it, then checks. Circle the tricky part in a bright colour. At breakfast, ask them to spell today's day name, and hunt for the d in Wednesday.","keyIdea":"Tricky words are common words that do not spell the way they sound, like said, was, come, friend and school. They are also called common exception words. Most of a tricky word still sounds out; usually one part breaks the rule, like the ai in said or the ch in school. Learn that part by heart with look, cover, write, check. The days of the week are tricky too: every one ends in day, and Wednesday has a d you do not hear.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each word sound out, or does it have a tricky part? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["sounds out","has a tricky part"],"items":[["cat",0],["said",1],["sun",0],["come",1],["went",0],["school",1],["shop",0],["friend",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagexcept","caption":"Flagship lab: fix the tricky part of sound-it-out spellings, look-cover-write-check, build the days of the week, fix sentences and sort word families.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_tHtjfjjFrl":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which way of adding ly to basic is right?","choices":["basically","basicly","basicaly"],"answer":0,"hint":"basic ends in ic. Words ending in ic add more than ly.","explain":"Words ending in ic add ally: basic, basically. publicly is the one common word that breaks this rule."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"vary + ous = ?","choices":["various","varyous","varous"],"answer":0,"hint":"vary ends in a consonant and y, and -ous starts with a vowel.","explain":"Change y to i, then add ous: various. varyous keeps the y and varous drops a letter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"extend + \"shun\" = ?","choices":["extension","extention","extenssion"],"answer":0,"hint":"extend ends in d. Which ending goes with d?","explain":"Roots ending in d take -sion: extend, extension. Watch out for attention and intention, which break the rule."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A word sounds like \"trezh-uh\". How is it spelt?","choices":["treasure","treature","treasher"],"answer":0,"hint":"zhuh at the end is spelt one way.","explain":"zhuh at the end is spelt -sure: treasure, measure, pleasure. -ture is for chuh, as in picture."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is spelt right?","choices":["teacher","teature","teacture"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a root word that ends in ch?","explain":"teach is a root word ending in ch, so just add er: teacher. -ture is for words like picture, with no root ending in ch."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A spelling list has {n} words. {k} of them end in -ture. How many do not?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the -ture words away from the whole list.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} words do not end in -ture.","params":{"n":[10,16],"k":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"permit + \"shun\" = ?","choices":["permission","permition","permision"],"answer":0,"hint":"Roots that end in mit take a double letter ending.","explain":"Roots ending in mit take -ssion: permit, permission and admit, admission."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Each day Maya learns {a} words with y for short i, like gym. How many does she learn in {d} days?","answer":"a*d","hint":"{a} words each day, for {d} days.","explain":"{a} × {d} = {=a*d} words, such as gym, myth and Egypt.","params":{"a":[2,5],"d":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"fame + ous = ?","choices":["famous","fameous","famus"],"answer":0,"hint":"-ous starts with a vowel. What happens to a last e?","explain":"The e drops before a vowel ending: famous. The ending is always spelt -ous, never -us."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which word is spelt right?","choices":["myth","mith","mythe"],"answer":0,"hint":"This word spells its short i sound with a different letter.","explain":"myth spells the short i sound with y, like gym and Egypt. These words have to be learnt."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which way of adding ly to gentle is right?","choices":["gently","gentlely","gentley"],"answer":0,"hint":"gentle ends in le. What happens to le before ly?","explain":"When a root ends in le, the le swaps to ly: gentle becomes gently. gentlely keeps both, which is a common slip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A list has {n} \"shun\" words. {t} end in -tion and {s} end in -sion. The rest end in -ssion. How many end in -ssion?","answer":"n-t-s","hint":"Take the -tion and -sion words away from the whole list.","explain":"{n} − {t} − {s} = {=n-t-s} words end in -ssion, such as expression and permission.","params":{"n":[14,20],"t":[4,7],"s":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"magic + \"shun\" = ?","choices":["magician","magition","magission"],"answer":0,"hint":"magic ends in c. Which ending goes with c?","explain":"Roots ending in c take -cian: magic, magician. A -cian word is usually a person with a skill."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben spells {a} -ly words and {b} -ous words in a test. How many suffix words does he spell?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the -ly words and the -ous words together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} suffix words, such as sadly and famous.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word has a short u sound spelt ou?","choices":["touch","cup","shout"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Which one sounds like u in cup but has ou?","explain":"touch sounds like tuch but is spelt with ou. cup uses u, and shout has the ow sound."}],"explain":{"kid":["A suffix is an ending you add to a root word. Add ly to sad and you get sadly. Some roots change first. happy turns its y to i: happily. gentle swaps le for ly: gently. basic adds ally: basically.","Some endings start with a vowel, like ous and ation. Then a last e drops. fame becomes famous. sense becomes sensation. vary turns its y to i: various.","The end of a word can sound like shun. Look at the root to spell it. invent ends in t, so we write invention. express ends in ss, so we write expression. extend ends in d: extension. music ends in c: musician. A musician is a person.","Listen to the very end. zhuh is spelt sure, as in measure. chuh is spelt ture, as in picture. teach ends in ch, so it is teacher. Some words just need learning: gym and myth have a y. young and touch have ou."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. The national curriculum for England sets these spellings for Years 3 and 4 (English Appendix 1). They are the suffixes -ly, -ous and -ation. Then come endings that sound like /ʃən/, spelt -tion, -ssion, -sion or -cian. Then /ʒə/ spelt -sure, /tʃə/ spelt -ture and /ʒən/ spelt -sion. Last are y for a short i and ou for a short u. The old rules for vowel suffixes still hold: drop the e, and change y to i.","The -ly rules: most words just add it. After a consonant, y turns to i if the root has two or more beats (happily, angrily). A root that ends in le swaps it for ly (gently, simply). A root that ends in ic adds ally (basically), and publicly is the one common odd word out. For /ʃən/, -tion is the most common spelling, and the root gives the clue. t or te gives -tion, ss or mit gives -ssion, d or se gives -sion, and c gives -cian. attention and intention break the rule. A -cian word is most often a person.","Common mistakes: gentlely and basicly; keeping the e in fameous; writing every /ʃən/ ending as -tion (musition, exprestion); spelling a root plus er as -ture (teature, not teacher); and spelling words the way they sound (mith, yung). The lab gives each slip its own feedback.","In the lab your child picks the -ly rule for eight roots. Next they tap the root letter that changes, or no change, for seven -ous and -ation words. They post twelve roots into the four /ʃən/ boxes by their last letters. They drag -sure, -ture, -sion or -cher into ten gaps from a spoken clue. Last, they fill y or ou into eight words."]},"familyTip":"Play root detective in the car or at tea. Say a \"shun\" word, such as invention, musician or permission, and your child names the root word and its last letters: invent ends in t, so -tion. Then swap: they say a root (act, magic, discuss), you build the word, and they check your spelling by the rule.","keyIdea":"A suffix is an ending added to a root word. -ly makes a how word: sad, sadly. Most roots just add ly, but y after a consonant turns to i (happily), le swaps to ly (gently) and ic adds ally (basically). -ous and -ation start with a vowel, so a final e drops (famous, sensation) and y turns to i (various). The ending that sounds like \"shun\" has four spellings, and the root's last letters choose: t or te takes -tion (invention), ss or mit takes -ssion (expression, permission), d or se takes -sion (extension, tension), c takes -cian (musician). \"zhuh\" is -sure (measure), \"zhun\" is -sion (division) and \"chuh\" is usually -ture (picture), unless the root ends in ch (teacher). Some words spell a short i with y (myth, gym) or a short u with ou (young, touch).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which \"shun\" spelling does each root take? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["-tion (t, te)","-ssion (ss, mit)","-sion (d, se)","-cian (c)"],"items":[["inject",0],["confess",1],["expand",2],["politics",3],["complete",0],["admit",1],["tense",2],["mathematics",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshun","caption":"Flagship lab: pick the -ly rule, change root letters for -ous and -ation, post roots into four \"shun\" boxes, match endings to sounds, fill y or ou.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_y8sicbhMci":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which spelling is right for a machine that washes dishes?","choices":["machine","mashine","masheen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Machine is a French word. How does French spell the sh sound?","explain":"machine uses ch for the sh sound and i for the ee sound, the French way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is the k sound in chorus spelt ch?","choices":["The word came from Greek.","Every k sound is spelt ch.","It came from French."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which language spells k with ch?","explain":"chorus came from Greek, and Greek words often spell k with ch. Kit and king do not, so not every k is ch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word ends with a k sound spelt que?","choices":["antique","attack","plague"],"answer":0,"hint":"que says k at the end of some French words.","explain":"antique ends que. attack ends ck, the English way, and plague ends gue, which says g."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which spelling is right?","choices":["science","sience","scince"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the silent c, and the vowels are i then e.","explain":"science is spelt sc-ie-nce. sience drops the silent c, and scince drops the i."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A word list has {s} sc words that say s and {k} sc words that say sk. How many sc words are there in all?","answer":"s+k","hint":"Add the s group and the sk group together.","explain":"{s} + {k} = {=s+k} sc words. The letter after sc tells you which group each one goes in.","params":{"s":[2,6],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In which word does ch say sh?","choices":["brochure","character","chair"],"answer":0,"hint":"French words spell the sh sound with ch.","explain":"brochure is French, so ch says sh. character is Greek, so ch says k, and chair has the everyday ch sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben writes uneek. What is the right spelling?","choices":["unique","unick","uniqe"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is French and ends with a k sound.","explain":"unique ends que. unick uses the English ck, and uniqe forgets the silent u."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why does sc say sk in scarf?","choices":["The next letter is a.","It is a French word.","sc always says sk."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the letter after sc.","explain":"After sc comes a, so the c is hard and sc says sk. Before e, i or y, sc says s, as in scene."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Each French word in a spelling test has {e} silent letters at the end, like the u and e in league. How many silent end letters are in {w} words?","answer":"e*w","hint":"Multiply the silent letters in one word by the number of words.","explain":"{w} × {e} = {=w*e} silent letters. You do not hear them, but you write them every time.","params":{"e":[2,2],"w":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which word should you spell with ch for the k sound?","choices":["echo","kitten","basket"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one came from Greek?","explain":"echo is Greek, so its k sound is spelt ch. kitten and basket are English words, spelt with k."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In which word does ch say k?","choices":["chemist","chin","chef"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word aloud. Which ch sounds like the k in kit?","explain":"chemist is from Greek, so ch says k. chin has the everyday ch sound, and chef is French, so ch says sh."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia sorts {n} ch words. {g} have a Greek ch that says k. The rest have a French ch that says sh. How many say sh?","answer":"n-g","hint":"Take the Greek words away from all the words.","explain":"{n} − {g} = {=n-g} words have a French ch, like chef or machine.","params":{"n":[6,12],"g":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the right spelling of a sports team group?","choices":["league","leeg","leage"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is French. The ending is gue with a silent u and e.","explain":"league uses ea for the long e sound and ends gue. leeg and leage drop the silent u and e."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A list has {a} words ending in gue and {b} words ending in que. How many silent u letters are in those endings?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Each gue or que ending has one silent u.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Every -gue and -que ending has one silent u, and one silent e too.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In which word does sc say s?","choices":["scene","scarf","scoop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the letter just after sc.","explain":"In scene, e comes after sc, so the c is soft and sc says s. In scarf and scoop, a and o come next, so sc says sk."}],"explain":{"kid":["Lots of English words came from other languages. Some still use the spelling from back then. If you know where a word came from, you can spell it.","In words from Greek, the sound k is often spelt ch. Think of chorus, echo and chemist. In words from French, the sound sh is often spelt ch. Think of chef and machine.","Some French words end with gue or que. League ends with a g sound. Unique ends with a k sound. You do not hear the u and e, but you still write them.","Words from Latin can have sc, like science and scene. Here sc says s. Look at the next letter. If it is e, i or y, sc says s. If it is a, o or u, sc says sk, like in scarf."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 10. English Appendix 1 of the national curriculum in England lists four patterns of foreign origin for Years 3 and 4: /k/ spelt ch (Greek: scheme, chorus, chemist, echo, character), /ʃ/ spelt ch (mostly French: chef, chalet, machine, brochure), /g/ spelt -gue and /k/ spelt -que (French: league, tongue, antique, unique) and /s/ spelt sc (Latin: science, scene, discipline, fascinate, crescent). Pupils use and extend them through Years 5 and 6.","The idea that ties these together is that the sound alone does not settle the spelling. The word's history does. Children learn to say the word, notice that ch is not making the chip sound, and ask which family the word belongs to. The sc rule is the same soft c rule as in city and cent: c before e, i or y says /s/, so the c in sc is silent there.","Common mistakes: writing the sound the plain English way, as in kemist, korus, shef or masheen; dropping the silent letters, as in leeg, leage or uneek; leaving out the silent c, as in sience or sene; and assuming sc always says /s/, which fails for scarf, scab and scoop. Some children also over-apply the new patterns and write words like baque for back, so ask whether the word sounds French or Greek before using them.","In the lab your child sorts twelve ch words by sound and builds six Greek and French spellings in a gap. They finish six -gue and -que endings and sort ten sc words by the letter that follows. Then they fix six misspelt words, each written the way it sounds."]},"familyTip":"Keep a three-column list on the fridge: Greek ch (k), French ch (sh) and French gue or que. When one of you spots a new word, such as orchestra, parachute or antique, say it aloud together and add it to the right column.","keyIdea":"English borrowed many words from Greek, French and Latin, and some kept the spelling habits of their first language. In Greek words the /k/ sound is often spelt ch, as in chorus, chemist and echo. In French words the /sh/ sound is often spelt ch, as in chef and machine, and a /g/ or /k/ sound at the end can be spelt gue or que, as in league and unique, with a silent u and e. In Latin words sc can say /s/, as in science and scene, but only when the next letter is e, i or y. Saying the word, then asking which pattern fits, turns a guess into a choice.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound does ch make? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["k (Greek)","sh (French)","ch (as in chip)"],"items":[["stomach",0],["parachute",1],["children",2],["orchestra",0],["moustache",1],["sandwich",2],["chaos",0],["chute",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagorigin","caption":"Flagship lab: sort ch words by sound, build Greek and French spellings, finish -gue and -que endings, test the sc rule and proofread.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HdI1y5KsBl":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The teachers all use one staff room. Which is right?","choices":["the teachers' room","the teacher's room","the teachers's room"],"answer":0,"hint":"teachers already ends in s.","explain":"The owner word is teachers. It ends in s, so add only the mark: the teachers' room."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The race is for women. Which is right?","choices":["the women's race","the womens' race","the womens race"],"answer":0,"hint":"women is already more than one woman.","explain":"women does not end in s, so it takes 's: the women's race."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence about pets is right?","choices":["We have three cats.","We have three cat's.","We have three cats'."],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the cats own anything in this sentence?","explain":"cats just means more than one cat. Nothing belongs to them, so there is no apostrophe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"'The {n} players' shirts' – each player has one shirt. How many shirts is that?","answer":"n","hint":"players' means all the players, not one.","explain":"The shirts belong to all {n} players, one each: {n} shirts.","params":{"n":[5,11]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam writes 'the boy's bikes' about five boys. What should he write?","choices":["the boys' bikes","the boys's bikes","the boy's' bikes"],"answer":0,"hint":"First write the owner word for five boys.","explain":"Five boys gives the owner word boys. It ends in s, so the mark goes after it: the boys' bikes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The mice share one hole. Which is right?","choices":["the mice's hole","the mices' hole","the mouse's hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"mice is the plural of mouse, and it has no s at the end.","explain":"mice is already plural and does not end in s, so it takes 's: the mice's hole. mouse's would mean just one mouse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does 'the birds' nest' tell you?","choices":["more than one bird owns the nest","one bird owns the nest","there are many nests"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everything before the mark is the owner word.","explain":"The owner word is birds, so more than one bird owns it. One bird would be the bird's nest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A farm has {h} horses. 'The horses' field' and 'the horse's saddle' are on a sign. How many horses own the field?","answer":"h","hint":"Look at where the mark is in horses'.","explain":"horses' means all the horses, so the field belongs to {h} horses. horse's is one horse.","params":{"h":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Where does the apostrophe go after a plural that ends in s?","choices":["after the s, with nothing added","before the s","after the s, then another s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the whole owner word first.","explain":"Write the whole plural, then the mark: girls', dogs'. No extra s is added."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Four girls share one tent. Which is right?","choices":["the girls' tent","the girl's tent","the girls tent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the owners first. Then write the owner word and add the mark.","explain":"There are four girls, so the owner word is girls. It ends in s, so the mark goes after it: the girls' tent."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} dogs. Each dog has 2 bowls. How many bowls are there in 'the dogs' bowls'?","answer":"n*2","hint":"The dogs' bowls means the bowls of all the dogs, not just one.","explain":"dogs' means more than one dog. {n} dogs × 2 bowls = {=n*2} bowls.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The coats belong to the children. Which is right?","choices":["the children's coats","the childrens' coats","the children' coats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the word children end in s?","explain":"children is already plural and has no s at the end, so it takes 's: the children's coats. There is no word childrens."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sign says 'the boys' changing room'. {b} boys and {g} girls are at the pool. How many people does the room belong to?","answer":"b","hint":"Look at the owner word before the apostrophe.","explain":"The owner word is boys, so the room belongs to the {b} boys, not the {g} girls.","params":{"b":[3,12],"g":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What does 'the cat's kittens' tell you?","choices":["one cat has the kittens","many cats have the kittens","the kittens are all cats"],"answer":0,"hint":"The word before the mark is the owner.","explain":"The mark comes straight after cat, so there is one cat. It can still have many kittens."}],"explain":{"kid":["You know that the dog's bone means one dog owns the bone. Now there are three dogs, and they share a kennel. We write the dogs' kennel. The mark goes after the s.","Here is the trick. Write the owner word first. Count the owners: three dogs, so the word is dogs. Then put the mark straight after it. dogs already ends in s, so stop. Do not add another s.","Some words mean more than one but do not end in s. Think of children, women, men and mice. They take 's, just like one owner: the children's toys, the mice's nest.","When you read, look at the word before the mark. That is the owner. The girl's bags means one girl. The girls' bags means more than one girl."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 10. In England, Year 2 pupils learn the apostrophe for one owner. Year 4 adds plural owners, such as girls' and boys' (English Appendix 2). Appendix 1 gives the rule. The mark goes after the plural form. If the plural already ends in s, no extra s is added. If it does not, add 's, as in children's.","The rule is two steps, always in this order. First, write the owner word as it should be: girl for one, girls for several. Second, put the mark straight after it. Add s only if the word does not already end in s. Reading works the same way backwards. Everything before the mark is the owner word, so it tells you how many owners there are.","Common mistakes: writing girl's when there are several girls; adding an extra s (girls's); making up a plural that does not exist (childrens', womens'); leaving the mark out (the womens race); and putting a mark on a plain plural that owns nothing (three apple's). Be careful when reading, too. The dog's bones can be many bones that all belong to one dog.","In the lab your child names four pictures by counting the owners. Then they drag the mark into the right gap in five owner words and pick the ending for four plurals with no s. Last, they read five phrases to say how many owners there are, and find the mistake in five sentences."]},"familyTip":"Next time you are out, point at things that belong to a group: the players' shirts, the ducks' pond, the children's playground. Ask your child to say the owner word out loud, count the owners, and then say where the apostrophe goes.","keyIdea":"To show that something belongs to more than one owner, write the plural owner word first, then add the apostrophe. If the plural ends in s, the mark goes after that s and nothing else is added: the girls' bags, the dogs' kennel. If the plural does not end in s, add 's, just as you would for one owner: the children's toys, the women's team, the mice's nest. To read a possessive, look at everything before the mark: that is the owner word, so the dog's bones belong to one dog and the dogs' bones belong to several.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"One owner or more than one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["one owner","more than one owner"],"items":[["the cat's bed",0],["the cats' bed",1],["the boy's coat",0],["the boys' coats",1],["the children's toys",1],["the teacher's desk",0],["the men's shoes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplposs","caption":"Flagship lab: count the owners, drop the apostrophe after the whole owner word, add 's to plurals with no s, read who owns what, and proofread.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_O2dS6gvClw":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which spelling of believe is right?","choices":["believe","beleive","belive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the hidden word lie.","explain":"Never believe a lie: b-e-l-i-e-v-e has lie in the middle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mo writes 'suprise'. What is missing?","choices":["an r after the u","a second p","an e after the s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it in a spelling voice: sur-prise.","explain":"surprise starts with sur. We often skip that r when we talk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which root word helps you spell knowledge?","choices":["know","now","ledge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word has the same meaning at its heart?","explain":"knowledge comes from know, so it starts with a silent k."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A spelling test has {q} list words. Mia gets {r} wrong. How many does she spell right?","answer":"q-r","hint":"Take the wrong ones away from all the words.","explain":"{q} − {r} = {=q-r} words spelt right.","params":{"q":[10,20],"r":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many letters s are in business?","choices":["three","two","one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it in a spelling voice: bus-i-ness.","explain":"bus-i-ness has one s in bus and two at the end: three in all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Things vanish. Which spelling is right?","choices":["disappear","dissapear","disapear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Build it from dis plus the word appear.","explain":"dis + appear: one s from dis and two p from appear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word in this sentence is spelt wrong? 'We went to the libary on Friday.'","choices":["libary","went","Friday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word slowly, the way it is spelt.","explain":"It is library: li-brar-y has two r."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is the best way to learn a hard list word?","choices":["find the hard part and use a trick for it","copy it out once","sound out every letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most of the word is easy. One part is not.","explain":"Find the part that breaks the rule and use a trick, like a spelling voice or a hidden word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word has just one m?","choices":["imagine","immagine","imagin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Not every middle letter is doubled.","explain":"imagine has one m: i-ma-gine, and it ends in e."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"Ben's story uses {s} list words. When he checks them, he finds {w} spelt wrong and fixes them. How many were right the first time?","answer":"s-w","hint":"Take the ones he had to fix away from all the list words.","explain":"{s} − {w} = {=s-w} list words were right before he checked.","params":{"s":[8,14],"w":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which spelling of the second month is right?","choices":["February","Febuary","Febrary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly in a spelling voice: Feb-ru-ar-y.","explain":"February has two r: Feb-ru-ar-y. We often skip the first r when we talk."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ella learns {n} list words a week. How many has she learned after {w} weeks?","answer":"n*w","hint":"Each week adds the same number of words.","explain":"{n} words × {w} weeks = {=n*w} words learned.","params":{"n":[3,6],"w":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which small word hides inside separate and helps you spell it?","choices":["rat","pear","ate"],"answer":0,"hint":"The hard part is the middle of the word.","explain":"sep-a-rat-e: there is a rat in separate, so the middle letter is a, not e."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A list has {t} words. Sam can spell {k} of them. How many does he still need to learn?","answer":"t-k","hint":"Take the words he knows away from the whole list.","explain":"{t} − {k} = {=t-k} words still to learn.","params":{"t":[20,30],"k":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word has a double letter?","choices":["address","imagine","calendar"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word in a spelling voice.","explain":"address has two d: ad-dress. imagine has one m and calendar has one l."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words are hard to spell, even when you know them well. Your school has a list of them for Years 3 and 4. February, separate and believe are on it.","Most of a hard word is easy. Only one part is hard. A trick for that part helps you remember it.","Trick 1: say it in a spelling voice. We say Feb-yoo-ary, but it is spelt Feb-ru-ar-y. Say each beat slowly, and you hear the r. Trick 2: find a hidden word. There is a rat in separate. Never believe a lie.","Trick 3: look for double letters. You hear one d in address, but it has two. Trick 4: find the root word. Knowledge comes from know, so it starts with a k you do not hear. After you write, check the list words. Read them slowly, the way they are spelt."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. English Appendix 1 of the national curriculum in England gives a statutory word list for Years 3 and 4. It has about 100 words that are often misspelt, such as accident, believe, different, February, knowledge, library and separate. Pupils should spell them, and the standards expect them to be used correctly in writing.","Most of each word is regular, so the job is to find the part that breaks the rule and learn that. Four strategies cover most of the list. A spelling voice (saying Feb-ru-ar-y or busi-ness as written) brings back letters that speech skips. A hidden word gives a hook (a rat in separate, a lie in believe, an ear in heart). Some words need a check for double letters (address, appear, opposite, occasion). For others, the root word gives the start (favour in favourite, appear in disappear).","Common mistakes: writing what you say, such as Febuary, libary, suprise and probly; swapping vowels, as in seperate and beleive; doubling the wrong letter or not doubling at all, such as adress, dissapear and imagine with two m; and spelling a word right in a test but wrong in a story. Careful: a hook only helps if it fits the hard part. A pear in appear does not tell you about the double p.","In the lab your child drags the letter we skip into six words, finds the hidden word in five more and chooses one letter or two in six. Then they pick the root word for five words and find the misspelt list word in five sentences."]},"familyTip":"Pick one list word a day, such as surprise or library. Ask your child to say it in a spelling voice, beat by beat, then write it without looking. Then make up a silly memory hook together for the hard part, like 'never believe a lie'.","keyIdea":"The Year 3 and 4 word list in England has about 100 words that children often misspell, such as February, separate, believe, address and knowledge. Most of each word follows the usual patterns; usually one part is hard. Four tricks help with that part: say it in a spelling voice to hear letters you skip when you talk (Feb-ru-ar-y), find a hidden word (there is a rat in separate), check for double letters (address has two d), and find the root word (knowledge comes from know). Then check those words in your own writing.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which trick helps with each word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["spelling voice","hidden word","double letter"],"items":[["February",0],["library",0],["separate",1],["believe",1],["address",2],["appear",2],["business",0],["island",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstatw","caption":"Flagship lab: say words in a spelling voice, find hidden words, choose one letter or two, find the root word and check a sentence.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XK7NYt61cO":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word has a silent k?","choices":["knee","kite","milk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the k you write but do not say.","explain":"knee sounds like nee. You hear the k in kite and milk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"I cut my ▢ on the tin. Which spelling is right?","choices":["thumb","thum","thumm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the b after m family: comb, lamb.","explain":"thumb has a silent b after the m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which family does wrist belong to?","choices":["wr","kn","mb"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first two letters.","explain":"wrist starts with wr. The w before r is silent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is there a b in doubt?","choices":["Writers added it to match the Latin dubitare.","People always said the b.","It shows the word is a verb."],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the word came from.","explain":"The b was never said in English. Scholars added it to show the Latin root, dubitare."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A story has {k} words with kn and {m} words with mb. How many words with a silent letter is that?","answer":"k+m","hint":"Add the two families together.","explain":"{k} kn words and {m} mb words make {=k+m} words with a silent letter.","params":{"k":[2,7],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word does NOT help you remember the g in sign?","choices":["signing","signal","signature"],"answer":0,"hint":"In which word is the g still silent?","explain":"In signing the g is still silent. You hear it in signal and signature."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The brave ▢ rode a horse. Which word fits?","choices":["knight","night","nite"],"answer":0,"hint":"A soldier, or the time when it is dark?","explain":"A soldier on a horse is a knight, with a silent k and gh. night is when it is dark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which letter is silent in autumn?","choices":["n","m","t"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly, au-tum, and listen for the last letter.","explain":"The n is silent. You hear it in autumnal: au-tum-nal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You spell {n} silent-letter words. You forget the silent letter in {f} of them. How many did you spell right?","answer":"n-f","hint":"Take away the words that lost their letter.","explain":"{n} words minus {f} slips leaves {=n-f} spelt right.","params":{"n":[8,12],"f":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which word is spelt right?","choices":["listen","lisen","lissen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the t you do not say, as in castle.","explain":"listen has a silent t after the s, just like castle and whistle. lisen and lissen spell it by sound alone."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which letter is silent in lamb?","choices":["b","m","l"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say lamb slowly. Which letter makes no sound?","explain":"lamb sounds like lam. The b after the m is silent."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and one of them is silent. How many letters do you say?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one you do not say.","explain":"{n} letters minus 1 silent letter leaves {=n-1} letters you say, just as knot has 4 letters but you say 3.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word helps you remember the c in muscle?","choices":["muscular","muscles","mussel"],"answer":0,"hint":"In which word can you hear a k sound?","explain":"In mus-cu-lar you say the c. muscles is the same word with -s, and mussel is a shellfish."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A spelling list has {w} words. {s} of them have a silent letter. How many words have no silent letter?","answer":"w-s","hint":"Take the silent-letter words away from the whole list.","explain":"{w} words minus {s} with a silent letter leaves {=w-s} with none.","params":{"w":[10,15],"s":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which spelling is right?","choices":["wrong","rong","whrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the w before r family: wrap, wrist.","explain":"wrong starts with a silent w before the r."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words have a letter you write but do not say. It is called a silent letter. Knot sounds like not, but it starts with a k.","Silent letters like to sit in the same pairs. A k or a g can hide before n, as in knit and gnat. A w can hide before r, as in wrist. A b can hide after m, as in comb.","Long ago, people said many of these letters. The way we talk changed, but the spelling stayed the same.","A family word can help you. You do not hear the g in sign, but you do hear it in signal. So you know sign has a g."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. The national curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Years 5 and 6) lists words with silent letters, such as doubt, island, lamb, solemn, thistle and knight. It says some of these letters were once sounded. This topic also asks pupils to use word families and word origins to remember the letter.","In Old English the k in knight and the w in write were said, and so was the b in lamb. The sounds dropped out of speech, but the spelling stayed. Other silent letters were never said in English. Scholars added the b in doubt to match the Latin dubitare, and the s in island came from a false link to isle. So a silent letter is not always a sound that was lost.","Common mistakes: spelling by sound alone (rite, thum, nife); putting the silent letter in the wrong place (kinfe); and treating an inflected form as a helpful relative. In signing the g is still silent, so only signal, signature or signify help. Watch for homophones too: knight and night, wrap and rap, and knot and not mean different things.","In the lab your child taps the silent letter in six words and sorts eight more into kn, wr, gn and mb families. They pick the relative that says the letter for six words, drag the letter back into six gaps, and find four slips."]},"familyTip":"Go on a silent-letter hunt in a book or around the kitchen (knife, comb, wrap). For each find, say the word in a funny 'spelling voice' that sounds the silent letter, like k-nife. Then try to think of a relative where the letter is really said, such as bomb and bombard.","keyIdea":"A silent letter is written in a word but not said, like the k in knot, the w in wrap, the b in lamb and the t in listen. Silent letters often sit in the same pairs: k or g before n (knit, gnat, sign), w before r (wrist) and b after m (comb, climb). Many were said in Old English and dropped out of speech, but stayed in the spelling. Others were added later to show where a word came from, like the b in doubt, from the Latin dubitare. A related word where the letter is said can remind you to write it: sign and signal, muscle and muscular, autumn and autumnal.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Where is the silent letter? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["at the start (kn, wr, gn)","after m (mb)"],"items":[["knock",0],["wrist",0],["gnome",0],["climb",1],["thumb",1],["comb",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagquiet","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the silent letter, sort kn, wr, gn and mb words, hear it in a relative, drag it back, fix the slip.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AhfyJoTQtY":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a tricky word?","choices":["was","cat","ship"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one does not match its sounds?","explain":"was sounds like woz but is spelt w-a-s. cat and ship match their sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you spell the end sound in duck?","choices":["ck","k","c"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sound comes straight after a short vowel.","explain":"Right after a short vowel like u, the k sound is spelt ck: duck, sock, back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many words are in: The dog is on the mat.","choices":["6","5","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count each word on a finger, even the small ones.","explain":"The, dog, is, on, the, mat: six words. Small words like is are easy to miss."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence is written correctly?","choices":["We had fun.","we had fun.","We had fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the start and the end.","explain":"A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop: We had fun."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How do you spell the long i sound in night?","choices":["igh","i","y"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a trigraph: three letters for one sound.","explain":"In night, light and high the long i sound is spelt igh, three letters for one sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sentence has {a} words and {b} of them are tricky words. How many are not tricky words?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the tricky words away from all the words.","explain":"{a} words take away {b} tricky ones leaves {=a-b} that follow the sound rules.","params":{"a":[5,8],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A word has {n} sounds. One of them is a digraph, two letters for one sound. How many letters does it have?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Every sound has one letter, but the digraph has one extra.","explain":"{n} sounds with one digraph gives {n} + 1 = {=n+1} letters. ship has 3 sounds and 4 letters.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What do you do first when someone says a sentence for you to write?","choices":["Say it back and count the words","Write the first letter quickly","Spell the hardest word"],"answer":0,"hint":"How do you hold a sentence in your head?","explain":"Saying it back and counting the words holds the whole sentence in your head before you start."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is the right spelling of they?","choices":["they","thay","thei"],"answer":0,"hint":"they is a tricky word. It ends in ey.","explain":"they is a common exception word: t-h-e-y. thay is a good try by sound, but not the spelling."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many sounds are in ship?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly. Is sh one sound or two?","explain":"sh is one sound made by two letters, so ship is sh-i-p: three sounds and four letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sentence has {a} words. Your child wrote {b} of them. How many words are missing?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the words written away from all the words in the sentence.","explain":"{a} words take away {b} written leaves {=a-b} missing. Counting words before writing helps stop this.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[3,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the right spelling?","choices":["said","sed","sayd"],"answer":0,"hint":"said is a tricky word. It does not match its sounds.","explain":"said sounds like sed but is spelt s-a-i-d. It is a common exception word to learn by heart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word has {n} sounds. Each sound gets one box. How many boxes do you draw?","answer":"n","hint":"One box for each sound, not for each letter.","explain":"{n} sounds need {=n} boxes. A digraph like sh still fits in one box.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How do you spell the /ai/ sound in the middle of rain?","choices":["ai","ay","a"],"answer":0,"hint":"ay usually goes at the end of a word.","explain":"In the middle of a word the /ai/ sound is usually ai (rain, tail). ay is used at the end (play, day)."}],"explain":{"kid":["A grown-up says a sentence. You write it. First say it back. Count the words on your fingers. Now you can hold it in your head.","Write one word at a time. Say the word slowly. Each sound gets its letters. ship has three sounds: sh, i, p. sh is two letters but one sound.","Some words are tricky. said does not look like it sounds. You learn tricky words by heart. Look, cover, write, check.","Then read it back. Is there a capital at the start? Is there a full stop at the end? Did you spell said and was the tricky way?"],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. The National Curriculum in England asks Year 1 pupils to write from memory simple sentences dictated by the teacher that include words using the grapheme-phoneme correspondences (GPCs) and common exception words taught so far, and Year 2 pupils to do the same with longer sentences and punctuation. Lower Key Stage 2 extends this to words and punctuation taught so far. In the US, Common Core L.1.2d and L.1.2e ask for conventional spelling of common patterns and frequent irregular words and phonetic spelling of untaught words, and L.2.2d asks children to generalise learned spelling patterns.","Dictation joins three skills. Holding the sentence is working memory: saying it back and counting words stops children dropping small words such as a, is and at. Segmenting is breaking a word into phonemes and choosing a grapheme for each one: a digraph (sh, ai, ck, oa) is two letters for one sound and a trigraph (igh) is three. Common exception words (said, was, they, the, have, come, one) have parts that do not match the taught GPCs, so they are practised as whole spellings.","Common mistakes: counting letters instead of sounds (s-h-i-p as four), writing ay in the middle of a word (rayn) or ck after a long vowel, spelling exception words by sound (sed, woz, thay), losing a small word from the sentence, and leaving out the capital or full stop because attention went on the spelling. A phonically plausible error like sed shows the child is segmenting well; the next step is to learn that word as an exception.","In the lab your child counts the words in four sentences, picks the grapheme for each sound in five words (ship, rain, duck, night, boat), chooses the right spelling of seven common exception words, hides a sentence and rebuilds it from memory three times, and finds the capital, full stop and tricky-word slips in four written sentences."]},"familyTip":"Say a short sentence about your day, like We had fish for tea. Your child says it back and holds up a finger for each word, then writes it. Do not show it. Read their writing together: did every word make it, is there a capital and a full stop, and are said or was spelt the tricky way?","keyIdea":"To write a sentence someone says to you, first say it back and count the words so you can hold it in your head. Then write one word at a time, saying each word slowly and putting one grapheme for each sound: ship is sh-i-p, three sounds. Some sounds need two or three letters (sh, ai, ck, igh, oa). Tricky words such as said, was and they do not follow the sound rules, so you learn them by heart. Last, read it back and check for a capital letter, a full stop and the tricky words.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you spell it by its sounds, or is it a tricky word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["spell it by its sounds","tricky: learn it by heart"],"items":[["ship",0],["said",1],["rain",0],["was",1],["duck",0],["they",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdictate","caption":"Flagship lab: count the words, spell each sound, pick the tricky words, write a sentence from memory and check it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9hBs430cU4":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which spelling of notice + able is correct?","choices":["noticeable","noticable","noticible"],"answer":0,"hint":"What sound should the c keep?","explain":"Keep the e so the c stays soft (like s). Without it, c before a would sound hard, like cap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which spelling of rely + able is correct?","choices":["reliable","relyable","relible"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a y after a consonant?","explain":"A y after a consonant turns to i before the ending: reli + able = reliable."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Adoration ends in -ation. Which is the matching adjective?","choices":["adorable","adorible","adoreable"],"answer":0,"hint":"An -ation cousin points to one ending. What happens to the e?","explain":"A related -ation word points to -able, and adore drops its e: adorable."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word breaks the root-word guide?","choices":["sensible","enjoyable","horrible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which has a whole root word but still takes -ible?","explain":"Sense is a whole word, yet sensible takes -ible. Enjoyable and horrible both follow the guide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Turn possible into an adverb. Which spelling is right?","choices":["possibly","possiblely","possibley"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the last letter changes.","explain":"Possible becomes possibly: the final e changes to y. Nothing is added."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A root word has {n} letters and ends in ce or ge, like notice. How many letters does its -able word have?","answer":"n+4","hint":"Is the e kept or dropped after a soft c or g?","explain":"The e stays to keep the c or g soft, so all {n} letters stay and able adds 4: {=n+4} letters (notice 6 → noticeable 10).","params":{"n":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A root word has {n} letters and ends in a consonant then y, like rely. How many letters does its -able word have?","answer":"n+4","hint":"The y changes to i. Does that change the count?","explain":"The y swaps for i, so the root keeps {n} letters, and able adds 4: {=n+4} letters (rely 4 → reliable 8).","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why can't you choose between -able and -ible by sound?","choices":["The endings sound the same when you say them.","-ible is always louder.","-able is only used in long words."],"answer":0,"hint":"Say comfortable and horrible. Listen to the endings.","explain":"The vowel in the ending is unstressed, so -able and -ible sound alike. That is why you test the root instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which spelling of the word meaning safe to eat is correct?","choices":["edible","edable","edibel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is ed a whole word with this meaning?","explain":"There is no whole root word in edible, so it takes -ible."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which spelling is correct?","choices":["dependable","dependible","dependeble"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cover the ending. Is depend a whole word?","explain":"Depend is a whole word, so it takes -able: dependable."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word ending in -able has {n} letters. How many letters does its -ably adverb have?","answer":"n","hint":"Does the adverb add letters, or swap one?","explain":"The final e swaps for y (comfortable → comfortably), so the adverb still has {n} letters.","params":{"n":[8,13]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Pick the correct spelling of the word that means easy to see.","choices":["visible","visable","visibel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is vis a word on its own?","explain":"Vis is not a whole word, so the ending is -ible: visible."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A root word has {n} letters and ends in e, like adore. You drop the e and add -able. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+3","hint":"Take one letter away, then add the four letters of able.","explain":"Dropping the e leaves {=n-1} letters, and able adds 4, so the word has {=n+3} letters (adore 5 → adorable 8).","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which adverb is spelled correctly?","choices":["terribly","terriblely","terribley"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the adverb add -ly, or swap the last letter?","explain":"Terrible becomes terribly: the e changes to y. Adding -ly to the whole word gives the wrong spelling terriblely."}],"explain":{"kid":["Say enjoyable and visible. The endings sound the same. So you cannot spell them by sound alone.","Try the root test. Cover the ending with your finger. Enjoy is a real word, so it is enjoyable. Vis is not a word, so it is visible.","Watch the join. Adore drops its e: adorable. Notice keeps its e, so the c still says s: noticeable. Rely changes y to i: reliable.","To say how something is done, swap the last e for y. Comfortable becomes comfortably. Possible becomes possibly. Be careful: possiblely is wrong."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. The National Curriculum in England lists words ending in -able and -ible, and -ably and -ibly, in English Appendix 1 for Years 5 and 6. The endings sound alike because the vowel is unstressed, so spelling needs a strategy.","The guide. -able is far more common. It is used when a complete root word can be heard (dependable, comfortable, reliable) and when there is a related word ending in -ation (adorable, applicable, tolerable). -ible is common when no complete root can be heard (possible, horrible, terrible). Roots ending in ce or ge keep the e before -able so the c or g stays soft (noticeable, changeable); forcible and legible use -ible instead.","Common mistakes. Children spell by ear and pick the wrong ending (horrable, dependible). They drop the e after a soft c or g (noticable) or keep it when it should go (adoreable). For adverbs they add -ly to the whole word (possiblely) instead of changing e to y. Words such as sensible, flexible, accessible, capable and probable break the guide and must be learned.","In the lab your child tests eight roots and spells six words by dragging an ending on. They fix the join for six roots and choose a or i for six words using -ation clues and soft c or g. They make five adverbs and sort eight words into follows the guide or rule breaker."]},"familyTip":"When your child meets a word ending in -able or -ible, ask them to cover the ending with a finger. Is a real word left? Enjoy is, so enjoyable. Vis is not, so visible. Then hunt for the rule breakers together: sensible, flexible and capable are good ones to know by heart.","keyIdea":"The endings -able and -ible sound the same, so test the root instead. If a whole word is left when you cover the ending, it is usually -able (enjoyable, dependable). If not, it is usually -ible (visible, possible). A related word ending in -ation also points to -able (adoration, adorable). At the join, most roots drop a final e (adorable), roots ending in soft ce or ge keep it (noticeable, changeable), and y after a consonant turns to i (reliable). For adverbs, swap the final e for y: comfortably, possibly. Some words break the guide, such as sensible, so check a dictionary when unsure.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which ending? Drag each root, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["-able","-ible"],"items":[["comfort",0],["terr",1],["wash",0],["incred",1],["reason",0],["invis",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagible","caption":"Flagship lab: test the root, drag on an ending, fix the join, use -ation clues, make -ably and -ibly adverbs, and sort the rule breakers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_T6nrrf2K43":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A word starts with the prefix dis- and has {h} letters in all. How many letters are in its root word?","answer":"h-3","hint":"Take the prefix off. dis has three letters.","explain":"{h} letters take away the 3 letters of dis leaves {=h-3} letters in the root word, which keeps its spelling.","params":{"h":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word means 'heat it before'?","choices":["preheat","reheat","misheat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which prefix means before?","explain":"pre- means before, so preheat means heat before. reheat means heat again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which spelling is right?","choices":["dissatisfied","disatisfied","dissatisfyed"],"answer":0,"hint":"dis ends in s. What does satisfied start with?","explain":"dis + satisfied keeps both s letters: dissatisfied. Nothing in the root changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An autograph is…","choices":["your own name, written by you","a graph about cars","a very big graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"auto- means self or own, as in autopilot.","explain":"auto- means self, so an autograph is your name written by yourself. It has nothing to do with cars."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word means 'against the way clock hands turn'?","choices":["anticlockwise","superclockwise","reclockwise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which prefix means against?","explain":"anti- means against, so anticlockwise means turning against the clock hands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word has the prefix re-?","choices":["rewrite","ready","reach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take re off. Which leaves a real word?","explain":"re + write = rewrite, write again. Take re off ready or reach and no word is left."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Jo wrote 'dissapear'. What went wrong?","choices":["She added an s: appear does not start with s","She forgot a p","Nothing: it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write dis, then write appear.","explain":"dis + appear = disappear, with one s and two p. The extra s is not in either part."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A root word has {h} letters. You add the prefix super- to the front. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"h+5","hint":"Count the letters in super, then add the root word's letters.","explain":"super has 5 letters and the root keeps all {h}, so the new word has {=h+5} letters.","params":{"h":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"mis + spell = ?","choices":["misspell","mispell","misspel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write all of mis, then all of spell.","explain":"Keep every letter: mis + spell = misspell, with two s where the parts meet."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What does mis- mean in misbehave?","choices":["wrongly","again","before"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of misread and misspell.","explain":"mis- means wrongly or badly, so misbehave means behave badly."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p6","prompt":"A root word has {h} letters. You add the prefix mis- to the front. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"h+3","hint":"mis adds three letters, and the root word keeps all of its letters.","explain":"The root keeps all {h} letters and mis adds 3, so the new word has {=h+3} letters.","params":{"h":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p7","prompt":"Sam reads a comic once. Then he rereads it {n} times. How many times has he read it in all?","answer":"n+1","hint":"re- means again, so each reread is one more read.","explain":"1 first read and {n} rereads make {=n+1} reads in all.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word does NOT have a prefix?","choices":["dish","dislike","disobey"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take the first letters off. Is a real word left?","explain":"Take dis off dish and you get h, which is not a word. dislike and disobey leave like and obey."}],"explain":{"kid":["A prefix is a small group of letters at the front of a word. It changes what the word means. The word it joins is the root word. Re + play is replay: play again.","Each prefix has a job. dis- and un- mean not: dislike, unfair. mis- means wrongly: misread. re- means again: redo. pre- means before: preheat.","Some prefixes are bigger. auto- means self: you write your autograph yourself. anti- means against: anticlockwise. super- means above or more: a superstar.","When you add a prefix, keep every letter. Mis and spell make misspell, with two s. Dis and appear make disappear, with one s. Some words just start with the same letters, like dish. Take dis off and h is not a word, so dish has no prefix."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 10. In England the Year 3 and 4 spelling appendix lists the prefixes un-, dis-, mis-, re-, sub-, inter-, super-, anti- and auto-, with the rule that most prefixes are added to the beginning of root words without any changes in spelling (examples: disappoint, misbehave, redo, superstar, antiseptic, autograph). Common Core L.3.4b and L.4.4b ask pupils to use a known affix as a clue to a word's meaning. This lesson adds pre-, which appears in the topic's list.","Meanings taught: dis- and un- mean not or the opposite of; mis- means wrongly or badly; re- means again or back; pre- means before; anti- means against; auto- means self or own; super- means above, over or more than. The spelling move is to write the whole prefix and then the whole root, which is why some words get a double letter at the join (misspell, dissatisfied) and others do not (disappear, mistake).","Common mistakes: dropping a letter at the join (mispell, disatisfied); adding one (dissappear, dissapoint); choosing a prefix with the wrong meaning (misagree for disagree); reading auto- as car because of 'autos'; and treating any word that starts with the same letters as prefixed (dish, ready, mister, reach). Teach the check: take the prefix off and see whether a real, related word is left."]},"familyTip":"Play prefix ping-pong: one person says a root word (like play, tidy or read), the other adds a prefix and says what the new word means. Then write the tricky ones together, such as misspell and disappear, and count the letters where the two parts meet.","keyIdea":"A prefix is a group of letters added to the front of a root word to change its meaning. dis- and un- mean not or the opposite (dislike, unfair), mis- means wrongly (misread), re- means again or back (replay), pre- means before (preheat), anti- means against (anticlockwise), auto- means self (autograph) and super- means above or more than (superstar). The root word keeps its spelling: write the whole prefix, then the whole root, so mis + spell = misspell and dis + appear = disappear. To check a word really has a prefix, take the letters off: a real word should be left (dish has no prefix, because h is not a word).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What does the prefix mean? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["not","again","wrongly"],"items":[["dislike",0],["unkind",0],["redo",1],["return",1],["misread",2],["misbehave",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpfx","caption":"Flagship lab: clip on dis-, mis-, re- or pre- to fit a clue, spell misspell and disappear, sort by meaning, match anti-, auto- and super-, and spot fakes.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
